<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-sufficient-criteria-for-series-convergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Sufficient Criteria for Series Convergence
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Before we are able to prove the <term> direct comparison test </term> in full, we establish the following special case:
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <lemma xml:id="lemma-comparison-of-non-negative-series">
    <title>
      Comparison of non-negative series
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ y_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be sequences of real numbers <m> x_{ n } , y_{ n } \in \R </m>, and suppose that <m> 0 \leq x_{ n } \leq y_{ n } </m> for all natural numbers <m> n \in \N </m>.
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } </m> converges, then so too does the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converge.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> diverges, then so too does the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } </m> diverge.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ S_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ T_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be the corresponding sequences of partial sums
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            S_{ n } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ k } } \amp T_{ n } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ y_{ k } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          S_{ n + 1 } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n + 1 }{ x_{ k } } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ k } } + x_{ n + 1 } \geq S_{ n }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>, so that <m> \of{ S_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-decreasing. Similarly, <m> \of{ T_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-decreasing. The <xref ref="theorem-monotone-convergence-theorem" text="title"/> implies that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } \amp = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ S_{ n } } = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ S_{ n } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } \amp = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ T_{ n } } = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ T_{ n } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Finally, we observe that
        
        <md>
          \sup_{ n \in \N }{ S_{ n } } = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ x_{ k } } } \leq \sup_{ n \in \N }{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ y_{ k } } } = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ T_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } </m> converges, then <m> \sup_{ n \in \N }{ T_{ n } } \lt \infty </m> is finite, and so
            
            <md>
              \sup_{ n \in \N }{ S_{ n } } \leq \sup_{ n \in \N }{ T_{ n } } \lt \infty
            </md>
            
            is finite as well. This implies that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges.
          </li>
          
          <li>
            If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> diverges, then <m> \sup_{ n \in \N }{ S_{ n } } = \infty </m> is infinite, and so
            
            <md>
              \infty = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ S_{ n } } \leq \sup_{ n \in \N }{ T_{ n } } \leq \infty
            </md>;
            
            that is, <m> \sup_{ n \in \N }{ T_{ n } } = \infty </m> is infinite as well. This implies that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } </m> diverges.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </proof>
  </lemma>
  
  <p>
    One consequence of <xref ref="lemma-comparison-of-non-negative-series"/> is that any series whose terms are bounded between the terms of two convergent series also converges. This result is called the <term> direct comparison test </term>, and it is one of the most useful tools in the analysis of series.
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-direct-comparison-test">
    <title>
      Direct comparison test
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>, <m> \of{ y_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>, and <m> \of{ z_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be sequences of real numbers <m> x_{ n } , y_{ n } , z_{ n } \in \R </m>, and suppose that <m> x_{ n } \leq y_{ n } \leq z_{ n } </m> for all natural numbers <m> n \in \N </m>. If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> and <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ z_{ n } } </m> converge, then so does the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ u_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ v_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be the sequences of real numbers <m> u_{ n } , v_{ n } \in \R </m> defined by the formulas
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            u_{ n } \amp = y_{ n } - x_{ n } \amp v_{ n } \amp = z_{ n } - x_{ n }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Then <m> 0 \leq u_{ n } \leq v_{ n } </m> for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>, and the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ v_{ n } } = \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \of{ z_{ n } - x_{ n } } }
        </md>
        
        converges by <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-convergent-series"/>. Thus the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } </m> converges by <xref ref="lemma-comparison-of-non-negative-series"/>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <example xml:id="example-applying-the-direct-comparison-test">
    <title>
      Applying the <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/>
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following are examples of applications of the <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/>:
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The series
              
              <md>
                \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } + n } }
              </md>
              
              converges by direct comparison with the convergent geometric series
              
              <md>
                \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } }
              </md>.
              
              Specifically, we use the estimate
              
              <md>
                0 \leq \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } + n } \leq \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } }
              </md>
              
              for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The series
              
              <md>
                \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ n^{ 2 } } = 0 + 1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25 + \dotsb
              </md>
              
              converges by direct comparison with the divergent series
              
              <md>
                \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ n } = 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + \dotsb
              </md>.
              
              Specifically, we use the estimate
              
              <md>
                0 \leq n \leq n^{ 2 }
              </md>
              
              for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    We now explore several corollaries of the <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/>. First, we note that our definition of absolute convergence in <xref ref="subsection-rearrangements-of-infinite-series"/> required convergence of two series; specifically, a series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges absolutely if both <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> and <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } </m> converge. We see by <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/> that we could equivalently only require the second of these series to converge.
  </p>
  
  <corollary xml:id="corollary-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence">
    <title>
      Absolute convergence implies convergence
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>. If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } </m> converges, then so does the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        We observe that (2) of <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-convergent-series"/> implies that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ -\abs{ x_{ n } } } </m> converges. Since
        
        <md>
          -\abs{ x_{ n } } \leq x_{ n } \leq \abs{ x_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>, the <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/> implies that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </corollary>
  
  <p>
    It is not always possible to easily make a direct comparison between the terms of a series and the terms of a convergent series. However, we may relax our estimates to asymptotic dominance relations and still obtain similar results.
  </p>
  
  <corollary xml:id="corollary-limit-comparison-test">
    <title>
      Limit comparison test
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ y_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be sequences of real numbers <m> x_{ n } , y_{ n } \in \R </m>, and suppose that <m> x_{ n } \preccurlyeq y_{ n } </m> as <m> n \to \infty </m>. If the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ y_{ n } } </m> converges absolutely, then so too does the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Since <m> x_{ n } \preccurlyeq y_{ n } </m> as <m> n \to \infty </m>, there is a sequence <m> \of{ \lambda_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of non-negative real numbers <m> \lambda_{ n } \geq 0 </m> and a non-negative real number <m> L \geq 0 </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \lambda_{ n } } = L
        </md>,
        
        and <m> \abs{ x_{ n } } = \lambda_{ n } \abs{ y_{ n } } </m> for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>. Consider the positive real number
        
        <md>
          \epsilon \gt \sup_{ n \in \N }{ \abs{ \lambda_{ n } - L } } \geq 0
        </md>.
        
        We observe that <m> L - \epsilon \lt \lambda_{ n } \lt L + \epsilon </m> for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \of{ L - \epsilon } \abs{ y_{ n } } \lt \abs{ x_{ n } } \lt \of{ L + \epsilon } \abs{ y_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>. (2) of <xref ref="proposition-arithmetic-of-convergent-series"/> and the <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/> imply that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } </m> converges. Finally, <xref ref="corollary-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence"/> implies that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </corollary>
  
  <example xml:id="example-applying-the-limit-comparison-test">
    <title>
      Applying the <xref ref="corollary-limit-comparison-test" text="title"/>
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } - 1 } }
        </md>.
        
        Since <m> \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } - 1 } \geq \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } </m>, we cannot easily directly compare this series to a convergent geometric series. However, we note that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \frac{ \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } }{ \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } - 1 } } } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \frac{ 2^{ n } - 1 }{ 2^{ n } } } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ 1 + \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = 1
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } - 1 } \sim \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } </m> as <m> n \to \infty </m>. In particular, <m> \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } - 1 } \asymp \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } </m>, and so <m> \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } - 1 } \preccurlyeq \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } </m> as <m> n \to \infty </m>. The <xref ref="corollary-limit-comparison-test" text="title"/> now implies that the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } - 1 } }
        </md>
        
        converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    We now briefly touch on the <term> alternating series test </term>, which does not follow from the <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/>. This convergence test deals with alternating series, which are those whose terms alternate in sign between positive and negative. We will see that it is much easier for such a series to converge, as there can be much cancellation in the partial sums.
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-alternating-series-test">
    <title>
      Alternating series test
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz
    </creator>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a non-increasing sequence of positive numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \opint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m>. If
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = 0
        </md>,
        
        then the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \of{ -1 }^{ n } x_{ n } } </m> converges.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ S_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be the corresponding sequence of partial sums
        
        <md>
          S_{ n } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ n }{ \of{ -1 }^{ n } x_{ n } }
        </md>.
        
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            S_{ 2 n + 2 } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ 2 n + 2 }{ \of{ -1 }^{ k } x_{ k } } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ 2 n }{ \of{ -1 }^{ k } x_{ k } } + \of{ -1 }^{ 2 n + 1 } x_{ 2 n + 1 } + \of{ -1 }^{ 2 n + 2 } x_{ 2 n + 2 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = S_{ 2 n } - x_{ 2 n + 1 } + x_{ 2 n + 2 } \leq S_{ 2 n }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        and
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            S_{ 2 n + 3 } \amp = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ 2 n + 3 }{ \of{ -1 }^{ k } x_{ k } } = \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ 2 n + 1 }{ \of{ -1 }^{ k } x_{ k } } + \of{ -1 }^{ 2 n + 2 } x_{ 2 n + 2 } + \of{ -1 }^{ 2 n + 3 } x_{ 2 n + 3 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = S_{ 2 n + 1 } + x_{ 2 n + 2 } - x_{ 2 n + 3 } \geq S_{ 2 n + 1 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>. So the subsequences <m> \of{ S_{ 2 n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> and <m> \of{ S_{ 2 n + 1 } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> are non-increasing and non-decreasing respectively, which implies that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ S_{ 2 n } } \amp = \inf_{ n \in \N }{ S_{ 2 n } } \amp \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ S_{ 2 n + 1 } } = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ S_{ 2 n + 1 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        by the <xref ref="theorem-monotone-convergence-theorem" text="title"/>. Since
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ S_{ 2 n + 1 } - S_{ 2 n } } } \amp = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ 2 n + 1 }{ \of{ -1 }^{ k } x_{ k } } - \sum_{ k = 0 }^{ 2 n }{ \of{ -1 }^{ k } x_{ k } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \of{ -1 }^{ 2 n + 1 } x_{ 2 n + 1 } } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ 2 n + 1 } } = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        <m> \of{ S_{ 2 n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> cannot diverge to <m> -\infty </m> and <m> \of{ S_{ 2 n + 1 } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> cannot diverge to <m> \infty </m>. Thus these sequences converge. The above computation also imply that these subsequences have the same limit, and so <m> \of{ S_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges; that is, the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \of{ -1 }^{ n } x_{ n } } = \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ S_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        converges.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <example xml:id="example-applying-the-alternating-series-test">
    <title>
      Applying the <xref ref="theorem-alternating-series-test" text="title"/>
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a positive real number <m> 0 \lt p \lt \infty </m> and the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty }{ \frac{ \of{ -1 }^{ n } }{ n^{ p } } }
        </md>.
        
        Since <m> \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ p } } </m> is decreasing and converges to <m> 0 </m>, the <xref ref="theorem-alternating-series-test" text="title"/> implies that the above series converges.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      We will see that when <m> 0 \lt p \leq 1 </m>, the series introduced in <xref ref="example-applying-the-alternating-series-test"/> are conditionally convergent; that is, these series are convergent but not absolutely convergent. The <xref ref="theorem-alternating-series-test" text="title"/>. The case <m> p = 1 </m> is of particular importance; it is called the <term> alternating harmonic series </term>.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
